-- title:
--   Weight-two cusp forms and periods are insensitive to -1
-- statement:
--   Let $\Gamma$ be a subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ of finite index, and write $\Gamma^{\pm} = \Gamma \sqcup \langle -1\rangle$ for the subgroup generated by $\Gamma$ together with the integer powers of $-1$. The assertion is that there exists a $\mathbb{C}$-linear equivalence $e$ from the space of weight-two cusp forms for $\Gamma^{\pm}$ to the space of weight-two cusp forms for $\Gamma$ with the following six properties. First, $e f$ and $f$ agree at every point of the upper half-plane, for every $f$ on the $\Gamma^{\pm}$ side; second, $e^{-1}g$ and $g$ likewise agree pointwise, for every weight-two cusp form $g$ for $\Gamma$. Third, for all $\tau_0,\tau_1 \in \mathbb{H}$ the dual map $e^{\vee}=\varphi \mapsto \varphi \circ e$ carries the segment-period functional of $\Gamma$ along $\tau_0,\tau_1$ — the functional $f \mapsto \int_0^1 f(\text{segment path from }\tau_0\text{ to }\tau_1\text{ at }t)\,(\tau_1-\tau_0)\,dt$ — to the corresponding functional for $\Gamma^{\pm}$. Fourth, for every $\gamma \in \Gamma$, $e^{\vee}$ carries $\mathrm{periodOf}\,\Gamma\,\gamma$, i.e. the segment-period functional along $i$ and $\gamma \cdot i$, to $\mathrm{periodOf}\,\Gamma^{\pm}$ evaluated at $\gamma$ regarded as an element of $\Gamma^{\pm}$. Fifth, whenever $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ is such that both $\gamma$ and $-\gamma$ lie in $\Gamma^{\pm}$, the periods of $-\gamma$ and of $\gamma$ in $\Gamma^{\pm}$ coincide. Sixth, for every $\varphi$ in the $\mathbb{C}$-dual of the weight-two cusp forms for $\Gamma$, $\varphi$ lies in the period lattice of $\Gamma$ (the $\mathbb{Z}$-span of all $\mathrm{periodOf}\,\Gamma\,\gamma$) if and only if $e^{\vee}\varphi$ lies in the period lattice of $\Gamma^{\pm}$.
--
--   This is the standard observation that $-1$ acts trivially in even weight, so that $S_2(\Gamma) = S_2(\Gamma^{\pm})$, packaged together with the resulting transport of segment periods, of the period functionals attached to group elements, and of the period lattice. It is used to replace a group by its $\pm$-saturation in the period-lattice computations for $\Gamma_H$, in particular in [`ModularCurve.petersson_mem_periodLatticeOf_iff_re_periodOf_int_gammaH`](thm.html#ModularCurve.petersson_mem_periodLatticeOf_iff_re_periodOf_int_gammaH) and in the description of the Abel–Jacobi multiplier as an exponential.
